      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj7",
      "function_role": "voice-leading-through-cycle-2",
      "narrative": "In Cycle 2 (stepwise root motion), maj7 voicings yield the tightest possible voice-leading motion: each upper voice moves by step, making this the ideal cycle for practicing minimal displacement across Cmaj7 and Fmaj7 in Close voicing. Use the Intervallic layer to confirm that no voice leaps more than a major second. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj7",
      "function_role": "voice-leading-through-cycle-5",
      "narrative": "In Cycle 5 (root motion by fourth/fifth), maj7 chords in C major connect Cmaj7 to Fmaj7 via dominant-resolution-style root motion; the major seventh of each chord resolves or is retained as a common tone in the next voicing. Consult the Functional layer to track how the leading tone function shifts across the cycle. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj7",
      "function_role": "msrp-top-voice-policy",
      "narrative": "Apply the M.S.R.P. string to the maj7 catalog entries to choose which chord tone occupies the top voice: the Root, Third, Fifth, or Seventh as soprano produces four distinct voicing shapes for Cmaj7 and Fmaj7. Cycling through all four MSRP positions across Cycles 2-7 generates the complete voice-leading matrix Goodrick encodes in the tables. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj7",
      "function_role": "parent-scale-context-harmonic-minor",
      "narrative": "Abmaj7 appears as the bVI maj7 chord in C harmonic minor; Goodrick's tables catalog its voice-leading through all six cycles in that context, where it functions as a tonic-substitute or pre-dominant sonority. Use the Functional layer entries for C harmonic minor to distinguish its role from the tonic-function Cmaj7 of C major. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
