      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "voicing-density-pairing",
      "narrative": "Min7 chords (Dm7, Em7, Am7 in C major) are cataloged in both Close and Spread voicings across all six cycles; in Close voicing the minor seventh collapses the voice-span, while Spread voicing opens the outer-voice interval, making common-tone retention more audible. Compare both density columns for the same cycle to identify which voicing density better sustains legato connections. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "voice-leading-through-cycle-5",
      "narrative": "Cycle 5 root motion chains min7 chords in a ii-V-like succession (e.g., Dm7 to Gdom7 to Cmaj7 in C major); the Almanac's tables isolate the min7 portion, showing how the flat-seventh of Dm7 descends by step to become the third of G7. Use this cycle's min7 tables as the foundation for constructing ii-V-I voice-leading frameworks. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "voice-leading-through-cycle-3",
      "narrative": "Cycle 3 (root motion by thirds) connects min7 chords that share common tones: Dm7 and Am7 in C major share C and E, so the Intervallic layer tables for Cycle 3 will show two stationary voices and two moving voices. Use this common-tone richness to practice smooth contrary-motion resolutions between third-related min7 chords. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "msrp-top-voice-policy",
      "narrative": "The four MSRP positions for min7 produce the root-position, first-inversion, second-inversion, and third-inversion voicing shapes; the Seventh-on-top shape places the minor seventh in the soprano, yielding the flattest, most open sound. Goodrick's tables enumerate all four for Dm7, Em7, and Am7 in every cycle and scale environment. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "parent-scale-context-melodic-minor",
      "narrative": "In C melodic minor, the diatonic min7 chords shift in quality relative to C major; Goodrick catalogs these altered diatonic positions so the player can compare how the same physical chord (e.g., Dm7) functions differently depending on its parent-scale context. Cross-reference the Functional layer entries between C major and C melodic minor tables to map common voice-leading pathways. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "voicing-density-pairing",
      "narrative": "G7 (dominant 7th) appears in C major, C harmonic minor, and C melodic minor (ascending variants), and Goodrick catalogs it in Close and Spread voicings; Close voicing concentrates the tritone (B-F) within a narrow span, while Spread voicing displaces it by an octave, changing the tension character. Always practice both densities for dom7 to control the intensity of the tritone resolution. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "voice-leading-through-cycle-5",
      "narrative": "Cycle 5 is the home cycle for dom7: the root-motion-by-fourth paradigm is the dominant-to-tonic resolution engine, and the Almanac's Cycle 5 dom7 tables encode exactly how each voice of G7 resolves into each MSRP position of Cmaj7. Use the Functional layer column to confirm that the tritone always resolves inward (B up to C, F down to E) regardless of voicing inversion. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "voice-leading-through-cycle-2",
      "narrative": "In Cycle 2 (stepwise root motion), dom7 chords appear consecutively in modes of the parent scale; the Intervallic layer tables show how minimal semitone or whole-tone shifts connect adjacent dom7 voicings, useful for chromatic parallel-dominant passages. This cycle reveals how much of the dom7 structure can be held static while the root descends by step. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "msrp-top-voice-policy",
      "narrative": "The Seventh-on-top MSRP position for G7 places the minor seventh (F) in the soprano, creating a falling-line potential into the third (E) of the resolution chord; the Third-on-top position places B in the soprano, creating a rising half-step resolution to C. Goodrick's tables catalog both soprano-voice scenarios so the guitarist can plan melodic direction through the dominant. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "parent-scale-context-harmonic-minor",
      "narrative": "G7 in C harmonic minor is the definitive V7 of a minor key; the Almanac catalogs its voice-leading in this context separately from C major, highlighting that the resolution target is Cmin (or CminMaj7) rather than Cmaj7. Use the harmonic-minor Functional layer entries to track how Ab (the b6 of C harmonic minor) appears in surrounding chords and shapes the approach to G7. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "functional-voice-leading-substitution",
      "narrative": "The Functional layer across cycles reveals that dom7 chords with the same tritone (e.g., G7 and Db7) share identical functional voice-leading profiles; Goodrick's exhaustive cycle tables allow direct cross-referencing so the player can substitute tritone-related dom7 voicings without relearning the resolution logic-only the root changes. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7b5",
      "function_role": "voicing-density-pairing",
      "narrative": "Min7b5 (half-diminished) chords-Bm7b5 in C major, Dm7b5 in C melodic and harmonic minor-are cataloged in both Close and Spread voicings; the diminished fifth between root and fifth is the defining interval, and Close voicing keeps it in its most compact, dissonant form. Compare densities to find which placement most clearly exposes the half-diminished color for ii-half-dim to V7 motion. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7b5",
      "function_role": "voice-leading-through-cycle-5",
      "narrative": "In Cycle 5, Bm7b5 resolves toward E7 (or Em) by fourth-root motion; the Almanac tables show how the diminished fifth (B-F) is absorbed into the next voicing with minimal displacement, and the Intervallic layer confirms whether the b5 moves up by half-step (to the natural fifth of the target) or held as a common tone. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7b5",
      "function_role": "parent-scale-context-melodic-minor",
      "narrative": "Dm7b5 occupies a different diatonic position in C melodic minor (IV) than Bm7b5 does in C major (VII); Goodrick catalogs both, enabling the player to see how the same chord quality takes on different functional roles depending on scale context. Cross-reference the Functional layer between the two scale tables to identify shared approach and departure patterns. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7b5",
      "function_role": "msrp-top-voice-policy",
      "narrative": "The b5-on-top MSRP position for min7b5 places the most dissonant interval in the soprano, maximizing tension before resolution; the Root-on-top position produces the least dissonant soprano line. Use the Almanac's MSRP layer to plan whether the half-diminished chord should peak or sustain the tension arc into the following dominant. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dim7",
      "function_role": "voicing-density-pairing",
      "narrative": "Bdim7 appears as the viio7 chord in C harmonic minor; because dim7 is fully symmetric (all minor thirds), its Close and Spread voicings are inversionally equivalent, and Goodrick's paired catalog entries reveal which voicing position is most fingering-efficient on guitar for each cycle. Use both density columns to build a complete set of enharmonic substitution voicings. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dim7",
      "function_role": "voice-leading-through-cycle-3",
      "narrative": "Cycle 3 (third-related root motion) is particularly revealing for dim7 because the chord's symmetry makes every minor-third transposition an enharmonic inversion of itself; Goodrick's Cycle 3 dim7 tables expose how the same four pitches rotate through different voicing positions as the 'root' changes. The Intervallic layer entries confirm that no voice actually moves-only the labeling shifts. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dim7",
      "function_role": "functional-voice-leading-substitution",
      "narrative": "The Functional layer for Bdim7 in C harmonic minor documents its role as a dominant substitute (viio7 = V7b9 without root); cross-reference these entries against the dom7 Cycle 5 tables to find equivalent resolution patterns. Goodrick's exhaustive enumeration makes it possible to swap Bdim7 for G7b9 in any voicing shape and identify the closest common-tone connection. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dim7",
      "function_role": "parent-scale-context-harmonic-minor",
      "narrative": "Bdim7 is exclusively cataloged within C harmonic minor in the Almanac (the only parent scale among the three that generates a diatonic dim7 chord); use this as a filter-whenever you encounter dim7 voice-leading tables in Chapter 1, you are operating in harmonic minor territory. The MSRP layer provides four soprano-position variants, all equally tense due to the chord's symmetry. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min-maj7",
      "function_role": "voicing-density-pairing",
      "narrative": "CminMaj7 (minor triad with major seventh) appears in both C melodic minor and C harmonic minor as the tonic chord; Goodrick catalogs it in Close and Spread voicings, and the major seventh creates an especially sharp clash against the minor third in Close density. Practice both densities to calibrate how much of the chord's characteristic tension is density-dependent versus inherent to the quality itself. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min-maj7",
      "function_role": "voice-leading-through-cycle-5",
      "narrative": "In Cycle 5, CminMaj7 as tonic receives resolution from G7 (V7 of C harmonic/melodic minor); the Almanac tables show how the major seventh (B natural) of the minMaj7 is approached from the leading tone of G7-they are the same pitch, making it a common-tone arrival. Use the Functional layer to confirm this leading-tone retention as the defining feature of minor-key tonic voice-leading. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min-maj7",
      "function_role": "msrp-top-voice-policy",
      "narrative": "The Major-Seventh-on-top MSRP position for CminMaj7 places B natural in the soprano, immediately signaling the raised seventh that distinguishes this chord from a standard min7; this soprano choice creates the most harmonically distinctive sound. Use Goodrick's MSRP tables to plan whether the raised seventh appears as a melodic goal or as an inner voice depending on the cycle context. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min-maj7",
      "function_role": "parent-scale-context-melodic-minor",
      "narrative": "In C melodic minor, CminMaj7 is the tonic chord and its voice-leading through all six cycles is cataloged; the Functional layer entries specifically trace how the major seventh (B natural) behaves differently in ascending melodic minor context versus harmonic minor context, since the surrounding scale degrees differ. Compare the C melodic minor and C harmonic minor minMaj7 entries side by side to identify which cycles produce identical voice motions. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "aug-maj7",
      "function_role": "voicing-density-pairing",
      "narrative": "EbMaj7#5 (aug-maj7, the III+ chord) is exclusive to C harmonic minor and is cataloged in Close and Spread voicings; the augmented fifth makes this one of the most internally dissonant major-seventh types, and Spread voicing distributes that dissonance across a wider register, making it slightly less abrasive while retaining the augmented color. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "aug-maj7",
      "function_role": "parent-scale-context-harmonic-minor",
      "narrative": "The aug-maj7 chord is uniquely generated by the harmonic minor scale (bIIIMaj7#5 = EbMaj7#5 in C harmonic minor); Goodrick's catalog provides voice-leading through all six cycles in this context alone. Use the Intervallic layer to observe how the augmented fifth (B natural, enharmonic Cb) creates oblique or contrary motion in adjacent voices when the root moves. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "aug-maj7",
      "function_role": "functional-voice-leading-substitution",
      "narrative": "The Functional layer for aug-maj7 in C harmonic minor shows its relationship to the tonic and mediant functions; because the augmented fifth (B natural) is the same pitch as the major seventh of CminMaj7, cross-reference both chords' functional entries to find common-tone substitution pathways between them. This allows the player to substitute EbMaj7#5 for CminMaj7 without disrupting the soprano line. Chapter 1, pp. 12-189.",
      "source_work_id": "almanac-vol-1",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "almanac-vol-1",
          "citation": "Chapter 1 pp.12-189"
        }
